					<outline text="&lt;/html&gt;">
					</outline>
				</outline>
			</outline>
			<outline text="docs/storage.md">
				<outline text="# Storage">
					<outline text="Every wpidentity installation has the option of providing user-level storage.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="#### How to">
					<outline text="1. Provide an object named &lt;i&gt;database&lt;/i&gt; in config.json. There's an example in &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/wpIdentity/blob/main/config.json&quot;&gt;config.json&lt;/a&gt; in this repo.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="2. In your MySQL database, create tables wpstorage and users with these commands. ">
						<outline text="```SQL">
						</outline>
						<outline text="&lt;%includeExample (&quot;createTable&quot;)%&gt;">
						</outline>
						<outline text="```">
						</outline>
					</outline>
					<outline text="3. If you put these tables in its own database you should create the database with:">
						<outline text="`create database wpidentity character set utf8mb4 collate utf8mb4_unicode_ci;`">
						</outline>
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="#### How it works">
					<outline text="We use WordPress for identity.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Each user has their own set of files. For example, you could keep a prefs.json file for each user, or a bookmarks.opml file. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="A file can be attached to a specific WordPress post, identified by site and post id's. This can be used for per-post prefs, or using a different kind of editor to write a post. It basically allows you to store data alongside a post. It could also be that the post is sort of a home page for a data set that's attached to the page. It's basically viewing WordPress as a database that has publishing built-in. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Files can be public or private. Most files are private. An example of a public file is the RSS feed we keep for each site that is updated via wpIdentity. This allows us to experiment with new feed features without having to change WordPress. ">
						<outline text="* An &lt;a href=&quot;https://wordland.social/scripting/41915670/rss.xml&quot;&gt;example&lt;/a&gt; from a test site. ">
						</outline>
					</outline>
					<outline text="The relpath field is meant to be a relative path to the &quot;home&quot; of the user's storage, but it's really just a string. You can use slashes to create structure, so for example there could be prefs files for different apps, with the first part of the path being the name of the app. The demo app stores its prefs file in demo/prefs.json. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="#### Queries that work">
					<outline text="Are now on &lt;a href=&quot;queriesthatwork.md&quot;&gt;their own page&lt;/a&gt;. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
			</outline>
			<outline text="docs/history.md">
				<outline text="# wpIdentity history">
					<outline text="How it came to be, to whom do we owe credit, and how to explore.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### What is wpIdentity?">
					<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/wpIdentity/tree/main&quot;&gt;wpIdentity&lt;/a&gt; simplifies the &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/Automattic/wp-calypso/tree/trunk/packages/wpcom.js&quot;&gt;WordPress REST API&lt;/a&gt;, making it easier to incorporate into JavaScript apps running in the browser. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="It has grown beyond its initial function to serve as a foundation for a simple browser-based writing app I'm working on, that works primarily with WordPress.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### FeedLand needed it, quickly">
					<outline text="It's called &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/wpIdentity&quot;&gt;wpidentity&lt;/a&gt;, because that's initially what its purpose was, to provide an easy way for a browser-based app use WordPress as an identity system. That functionality is used in &lt;a href=&quot;https://feedland.org/&quot;&gt;FeedLand&lt;/a&gt;. It was kind of an emergency project because Twitter, which we had previously used for identity was going through its own identity crisis. As a result FeedLand uses WP identity by default, in addition to email-based identity which serves as a backup against any future problems.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Foundation for browser-based apps">
					<outline text="It does much more than identity. The thing I'm most proud of is the simplicity of the API. WordPress already has a very nice REST API but it's possible to further factor the API by creating an object called wordPress which is defined in a file called &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/wpIdentity/blob/main/client/api2.js&quot;&gt;api2.js&lt;/a&gt; in the &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/wpIdentity/tree/main/client&quot;&gt;client folder&lt;/a&gt; of the wpidentity project, which is a very simple demo app. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="This API is patterned after the approach Facebook took in their browser interface, which I first used about ten years go when I was working on Fargo. I was very impressed with how much easier it was to use than the Twitter API which works more or less like the WordPress API does. They all get the job done, but the FB approach factors out a lot of the code you'd have to write if you were using the &quot;naked&quot; WordPress API. (It's possible others have done this previously, not claiming to have invented this, I want to be sure people get that.)">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### WordLand (November 2024)">
					<outline text="With &lt;a href=&quot;https://this.how/wordland/&quot;&gt;WordLand&lt;/a&gt; it also gets a &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/wpIdentity/blob/main/wpidentity.js#L423&quot;&gt;storage&lt;/a&gt; component, because WordLand needs to store drafts and user preferences. And may want to implement more storage functions in the future. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Also the wpcom interface is slower, since we save the draft as the user types, it needs to be pretty fast. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Missing functionality" isComment="true">
					<outline text="I still have some more functionality to cover, notably uploads of media objects.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### License">
					<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/wpIdentity&quot;&gt;wpIdentity&lt;/a&gt; is licensed under the most liberal MIT license. So if Automattic or the foundation or whoever (I'm confused about who's who in this world) wants to adopt it, they can -- or any developer is free to use it however they like. As a personal favor, I would like to be credited personally as the initial author if you choose to use it, and please point back to my GitHub project. But you are not &lt;i&gt;required&lt;/i&gt; to do all this. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Why now?">
					<outline text="Anyway, I find myself wanting to explain this to a few people now, in Oct 2024, so I thought it would be best to write it up, and answer any questions people might have, to improve the docs. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
			</outline>
			<outline text="docs/queriesthatwork.md">
				<outline text="select id, username, relpath, type, idSite, idPost, whenCreated, whenUpdated, ctSaves from wpstorage order by whenUpdated desc;">
				</outline>
				<outline text="select filecontents from wpstorage where id = 65; ">
				</outline>
				<outline text="select filecontents from wpstorage where relpath = 'bingeworthy/profile.json';">
				</outline>
				<outline text="select id, username, whenCreated, whenUpdated, ctSaves from wpstorage where relpath = 'wordland/prefs.json' order by ctSaves desc limit 25;">
				</outline>
				<outline text="select * from log order by id desc limit 100;">
				</outline>
				<outline text="select id, eventName, whenCreated from log order by id desc limit 100;">
				</outline>
				<outline text="select id, eventData from log order by id desc limit 100;">
				</outline>
				<outline text="select id, whenCreated, whenUpdated from wpstorage where username = 'scripting' and relpath = 'wordland/prefs.json' and flprivate = 1;">
				</outline>
				<outline text="select * from wpstorage where username = 'scripting' and relpath = 'wordland/prefs.json' and flprivate = 1\G">
				</outline>
				<outline text="select filecontents from wpstorage where username = 'scripting' and relpath = 'wordland/prefs.json' and flprivate = 1\G">
				</outline>
				<outline text="select id, whenCreated from wpstorage where username = 'scripting' and relpath = 'wordland/prefs.json' and flprivate = 1;">
				</outline>
				<outline text="select filecontents from wpstorage where id = 5681;">
				</outline>
			</outline>
			<outline text="readme.md">
				<outline text="# wpIdentity">
					<outline text="A &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.npmjs.com/package/wpidentity&quot;&gt;package&lt;/a&gt; that implements &lt;a href=&quot;https://developer.wordpress.com/docs/wpcc/&quot;&gt;OAuth identity&lt;/a&gt; and a simple verb set with wordpress.com for Node.js apps.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="It's used in FeedLand for WordPress identity and it's the complete backend for WordLand.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="In October 2024, I wrote a brief doc about the &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/wpIdentity/blob/main/docs/history.md&quot;&gt;history of wpIdentity&lt;/a&gt;, its purpose, where it came from, how to explore. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Demo apps">
					<outline text="* A basic &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/wpIdentity/tree/main/client&quot;&gt;hello world&lt;/a&gt; type demo. You can log on, and see a list of your sites, when each was created and modified. You can try it out &lt;a href=&quot;http://scripting.com/code/wpidentity/client/&quot;&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;a href=&quot;https://imgs.scripting.com/2023/09/05/wpsitelist.png&quot;&gt;Screenshot&lt;/a&gt;.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="* A simple &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/wpEditorDemo/blob/main/README.md&quot;&gt;Markdown editor&lt;/a&gt; that publishes to WordPress sites. All the basic features with easy-to-read and crib code from. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="* A &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/wpEditorDemo/blob/main/apps/gutenberg/README.md&quot;&gt;variant&lt;/a&gt; of the Markdown editor, above, that edits posts in Gutenberg. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="* &lt;a href=&quot;https://wordland.social/&quot;&gt;WordLand&lt;/a&gt;, an editor that's designed for social applications built on a wpIdentity backend. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### How to set up a server">
					<outline text="1. Download the folder.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="2. Throw away everything but the shell folder. In the shell folder you can throw away source.opml.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="3. Move the shell folder to your server.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="4. Edit the &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/wpIdentity/blob/main/shell/config.json&quot;&gt;config.json&lt;/a&gt; file with the credentials you got from WordPress.com. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="4. npm install">
					</outline>
					<outline text="5. node wpidentity.js">
					</outline>
					<outline text="6. If you want to use the storage feature, you'll need to &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/wpIdentity/blob/main/docs/storage.md&quot;&gt;set up the database&lt;/a&gt;. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Check out worknotes">
					<outline text="I've been narrating my work in the &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/wordpressIdentity/blob/main/worknotes.md&quot;&gt;worknotes&lt;/a&gt;. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Demo apps" isComment="true">
					<outline text="There's a very simple &lt;a href=&quot;http://scripting.com/code/wpidentity/client/&quot;&gt;demo app&lt;/a&gt; in the &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/wpIdentity/tree/main/client&quot;&gt;client folder&lt;/a&gt; here. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="In November 2025 I wrote a &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/wpEditorDemo&quot;&gt;simple editor&lt;/a&gt; that works through wpIdentity. ">
						<outline text="* This is &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/wpEditorDemo/blob/main/code.js&quot;&gt;code&lt;/a&gt; you can copy if you have an editor and want to configure it to work alongside WordLand.  ">
						</outline>
						<outline text="* You can also &lt;a href=&quot;https://demo.wpidentity.org/&quot;&gt;try out the editor&lt;/a&gt; here. ">
						</outline>
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Automattic docs">
					<outline text="These docs proved useful at different points in this project.">
						<outline text="* https://github.com/Automattic/wpcom.js/tree/master/docs -- docs for the routines we call ">
						</outline>
						<outline text="* https://developer.wordpress.com/apps/">
						</outline>
						<outline text="* https://developer.wordpress.com/docs/oauth2/">
						</outline>
						<outline text="* https://github.com/Automattic/node-wpcom-oauth">
						</outline>
						<outline text="* https://github.com/Automattic/wp-calypso">
						</outline>
						<outline text="* https://github.com/Automattic/wp-calypso/tree/trunk/packages/wpcom.js">
						</outline>
						<outline text="* https://github.com/Automattic/wp-calypso/tree/trunk/packages/wpcom.js/docs">
						</outline>
						<outline text="* https://github.com/Automattic/wpcom-connect-examples">
						</outline>
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Other pointers">
					<outline text="I am using this site for testing. https://daveverse.org/.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
			</outline>
			<outline text="config.json" isComment="true">
				<outline text="{">
					<outline text="{">
						<outline text="&quot;urlServer&quot;: &quot;https://myserver.org/&quot;,">
						</outline>
						<outline text="">
						</outline>
						<outline text="&quot;clientId&quot;: 12345,">
						</outline>
						<outline text="&quot;clientSecret&quot;: &quot;xxx&quot;,">
						</outline>
						<outline text="&quot;urlRedirect&quot;: &quot;https://myserver.org/callbackFromWordpress&quot;,">
						</outline>
						<outline text="&quot;scope&quot;: &quot;global&quot;,">
						</outline>
						<outline text="">
						</outline>
						<outline text="&quot;database&quot;: {">
							<outline text="&quot;host&quot;: &quot;databaseserver.org&quot;,">
							</outline>
							<outline text="&quot;port&quot;: 12345,">
							</outline>
							<outline text="&quot;user&quot;: &quot;dbadmin&quot;,">
							</outline>
							<outline text="&quot;password&quot;: &quot;xxx&quot;,">
							</outline>
							<outline text="&quot;charset&quot;: &quot;utf8mb4&quot;,">
							</outline>
							<outline text="&quot;connectionLimit&quot;: 15,">
							</outline>
							<outline text="&quot;database&quot;: &quot;wpidentity&quot;,">
							</outline>
							<outline text="&quot;debug&quot;: false,">
							</outline>
							<outline text="&quot;flLogQueries&quot;: false,">
							</outline>
							<outline text="&quot;flQueueAllRequests&quot;: false">
							</outline>
							<outline text=" }">
							</outline>
						</outline>
						<outline text="}">
						</outline>
					</outline>
				</outline>
			</outline>
		</outline>
		<outline text="build script">
			<outline text="nodeEditorSuite.utilities.buildWpIdentity ()">
			</outline>
		</outline>
		</body>
	</opml>
